JS slice for array
The slice() method in js is used to return a shallow copy of a portion of an array into a new array object,
without modifying the original array. You can specify a start index and an optional end index to determine the
section of the array you want to copy.

array.slice(startIndex, endIndex)
startIndex: The zero based index at which to start the extraction, if it is negative, it counts from the end
of the array (eg -1 is the last element)
endIndex(optional): The zero based index before which to end the extraction, if omitted, it slices until the end
or the array, if negative it counts from the end

The slice() method does not modify the original array, it returns a new array
If startIndex is greater than the array length, an empty array is returned
If endIndex is greater than the array length, it simply slices until the end of array

let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let result = arr.slice(1, 4);
console.log(result); // Output: [2, 3, 4]


let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let result = arr.slice(2);
console.log(result); // Output: [3, 4, 5]


let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let result = arr.slice(-3, -1);
console.log(result); // Output: [3, 4]


When you want to copy an entire array (use slice() with no arguments).